// ========================================================================
// Executive Summary
// ========================================================================

// This is the fixed base meta-model for NINJA, the rigorous digital
// engineering methodology formerly known as RDE.  NINJA is a graduated,
// incrementally-adoptable formal-assurance methodology that ties together
// Design-by-Contract, refinement chains, and machine-checked verification
// so that a claim made at the most abstract level of a system can be
// traced, refinement by refinement, all the way down to attested evidence
// produced by analysis and verification tools.
//
// The meta-model is realized in practice by HOARDE (Hierarchical
// Orchestration of Autonomous Rigorous Digital Entities), Sigil Logic's
// platform of orchestrated AI agents that author, refine, and check the
// artifacts described here.  Each sub-domain of the methodology is captured
// as an abstract Clafer "type structure" in a dedicated module and then
// concretized by a particular project (for example, the Needham-Schroeder
// pedagogical example that vendors this prelude).
//
// The modules of the fixed base meta-model are:
//   - prelude.cfr        traceability core + programming/specification
//                        language taxonomies (this file)
//   - platform.cfr       the development and target platform, including
//                        the AI inference substrate HOARDE runs on
//   - environment.cfr    the NINJA build, DevSecOps, and formal-methods
//                        toolchains
//   - specifications.cfr the kinds of specifications and their languages
//   - requirements.cfr   informal / semi-formal / formal requirements
//   - refinements.cfr    refinement relationships and their rigor
//   - architecture.cfr   generic system/subsystem/component architecture
//   - assurancecase.cfr  the assurance-case meta-model (CLARISSA + ETB)
//   - product.cfr        the product under development and its BOMs
//   - configuration.cfr  build system, CI, CD, and CV
//   - system.cfr         the top-level composition of all of the above
//
// Example constraints and assertions used to reason about these models and
// their inter-relationships are provided throughout.  The natural-language
// meaning of each proposition is stated adjacent to its formal
// specification.

// ========================================================================
// We start with a few placeholder concepts, which will be refined later.
// ========================================================================

// Nothing here right now.

// ========================================================================
// Here are some generic "types" of Clafers. Some types do not have
// properties, but are rather used for readability for the user.
// ========================================================================

abstract VersionInfo
  major_version -> integer
  minor_version -> integer
  [ 0 <= major_version
    0 <= minor_version ]

// ========================================================================
// Traceability
// ========================================================================

// A URL is a value-carrying trace *handle*.  An element may trace to more
// than one location, but Chocosolver cannot hold a set of more than one
// string on a single string reference (it int-encodes strings).  So a URL
// is modeled as a handle instance carrying its own single-valued string
// `value`, and elements reference a *set* of URL handles.  This is the
// hybrid recommended by the string-handling variant study (see the module
// README): use handles ONLY for the genuinely set-valued `url` field, and
// keep every other trace field (`name`, `referrant`, `id`, `text`) a plain
// single-valued string.  Because the handle carries its `value` in-model,
// the URL text remains machine-visible (no unchecked comment-only side
// table), unlike a pure-handle representation.
abstract URL
  value -> string

// A named element is any concept that has human-readable name(s).
abstract NamedElement
  name -> string ?
  version: VersionInfo ?

// A traceable element is a concept that carries the traceability triple:
// a set of (persistent) `url`s pointing at the artifacts it corresponds
// to, a `referrant` index into those artifacts (a section or table number,
// or a fully-resolved declaration in a specification or program), and, when
// no such binder is available, a `line_no`.  `url` is the one genuinely
// set-valued trace field (an element may trace to several locations), so it
// is a set of value-carrying `URL` handles; `referrant` stays a plain
// string.  Note: `url`/`referrant`/`line_no` live here on TraceableElement,
// NOT on NamedElement, so that merely-named things (tools, languages) do
// not carry trace data they never use.
abstract TraceableElement: NamedElement
  url -> URL *
  referrant -> string
  line_no -> integer
  [ 1 <= line_no ]

nothing: TraceableElement
  [ referrant = "TBD" ]

// ========================================================================
// Programming Languages Metamodel
// ========================================================================

// A list of all of the programming (implementation and synthesis)
// languages that we regularly use in NINJA projects, expressed as a
// Clafer enumeration.  Languages that also serve as specification or
// proof languages (e.g. F* and Dafny) carry the `ProgLang` facet here and
// a corresponding `SpecLanguage` member below.  (Interactive-proof
// assistants such as Coq, Lean, Isabelle, PVS, and Agda are modeled purely
// as specification/proof languages and as `FormalMethodsTool`s, not as
// `ProgLanguage`s.)
enum ProgLanguage = Ada | C | CPlusPlus | CSharp | Dafny | FStarProgLang | Go | Haskell | Java | JavaScript | Kotlin | OCaml | Python | Rust | SPARK | Scala | Slang | Swift | TypeScript

// ========================================================================
// Specification Languages Metamodel
// ========================================================================

// A list of all of the specification, modeling, proof, and property
// languages that we regularly use in NINJA projects, expressed as a
// Clafer enumeration.  This covers the full HOARDE toolchain: domain
// engineering (Lando/EBON, VDM-SL), feature modeling (Clafer, BigGears),
// code contracts (JML, ACSL), cryptographic specification (Cryptol),
// protocol analysis (Tamarin, ProVerif, SAPIC(+)), model checking
// (Promela, TLA+, mCRL2/CSP), interactive proof (Coq, Lean, Isabelle,
// PVS, F*), constraint solving (SMT-LIB, Alloy), requirements (FRETish,
// Spear, ReqML, DOORS), and evidence orchestration (Datalog, for ETB).
enum SpecLanguage = AADL | ACSL | ASCII | ASMLang | AlloyLang | BigGears | CSPm | ClaferLang | CoqLang | CryptolLang | DOORSLang | Datalog | EBONLang | EventB | Excel | FRETish | FStarSpecLang | IsabelleLang | JMLLang | LTL | LandoLang | LeanLang | PVSLang | Promela | ReqML | SALLang | SAPIC | SAPICStar | SAWScript | SMTLIB | Spear | StateCharts | SysMLv1 | SysMLv2 | TLAPlus | TamarinLang | UML | VDMSL | Word | ZNotation

// The assurance-case meta-model, formerly a placeholder here, now lives
// in its own module `assurancecase.cfr` (a CLARISSA/Assurance 2.0
// structured assurance case integrated with SRI's Evidential Tool Bus).
